I now understand the slot 3 mission clearly. Let me build a real Hardy-Z-based zero-finder using the FFT-shared L-eval primitive from slot 2, then measure end-to-end π(x; q, a) cost.

Key design decisions:
1. Full AFE = main term + reflected term. The reflected term is `W_χ · (q/π)^{-it} · ratio_gamma · conj(main term)` — no extra FFT needed beyond slot 2's primitive.
2. Hardy Z: `Z_χ(t) = e^{iθ_χ(t)} · L(½+it, χ)` real on critical line; sign-change bracketing + linear interpolation refinement.
3. For prime q, χ_j is even iff j is even. Gauss sums τ(χ_j) come from a single length-φ FFT (same trick as slot 2).
