    866	| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 2 — AFE-shared L-value evaluation across characters mod q via cyclic DFT identity over (Z/qZ)*: does sharing the `1/n^{1/2+it}` table across all chi mod q give meaningful per-character cost reduction over optimised direct BLAS matmul? | REFINEMENT (operational confirmation + bounded partial-positive) | I (slot-2 falsifier α confirmed; wall-clock benefit bounded constant; slot 3-5 attack adjacent angles) | Thread 6 commit step 2/5 (S228). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot2_afe_shared_l_eval.py` — three implementations of truncated AFE main term `L(1/2+it, chi) ≈ Σ_{n ≤ N, gcd(n,q)=1} chi(n) / n^{1/2+it}`: DIRECT (`chi_at_n @ cp` via vectorised `char_table_at_residues` building only φ × N_co columns); AGGREGATE_MATMUL (build W[r, j] aggregate then `char_table @ W` of shape φ × φ — strictly worse than DIRECT at N_co < φ, skipped at q ≥ 5000 due to φ² memory); FFT (aggregate W in log-g order, then `phi · ifft(W, axis=0)` exploits cyclic DFT identity `χ_j(g^k) = ω^{jk}` to compute L for ALL φ chars at once per t). **Cross-method correctness**: max relative error vs DIRECT is ~1e-15 across q ∈ {11, 31, 101} (numerical floating-point limit). **HONEST cost profile** (12 rows q ∈ {101, 1009, 10007} × t_max ∈ {50, 200} × n_t ∈ {400, 800}): FFT wins in 11/12 configurations with median speedup 1.7× over OPTIMISED DIRECT, range 0.74×-6.35×. **Scaling profile** (q ∈ {101, 251, 503, 1009, 2003, 5003}, t_max=100, n_t=400): empirical FFT speedup is ESSENTIALLY FLAT in q at ~1.7×, while theoretical FLOP-ratio `N_co/log φ` grows from 5.8× (q=101) to 22.9× (q=5003). **Gap = BLAS hardware FLOP rate (~30 GFLOP/s, well-optimised) vs np.fft Bluestein (~3-5 GFLOP/s) — ratio ~8-12×**. End-to-end pi(x; q, a) implication: single-query speedup ~5× vs slot-1 synthetic baseline (188ms → ~33ms at q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶), but amortised regime UNCHANGED at slot-1 T_full_per_x = 22.7ms (set by phi(q) · K · 50ns partial-sum eval, which slot-2 primitive does NOT affect). **NOT a Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped partial-positive** (not the 33×-shaped result Thread 5 produced). Slot-1 falsifier (α) is structurally CORRECT but practically WEAK: cyclic DFT identity gives the right algebraic primitive, but BLAS hardware efficiency closes the wall-clock gap. **Methodological correction**: replaced slot-1-inherited `all_characters_table()` (φ² Python double loop, O(100s) overhead at q=10007) with vectorised `char_table_at_residues(q, residues)` (np.exp over outer-product (φ, len(residues))). Affects all future χ-side experiments. **Falsifier list F2.1-F2.4 framing slot 3+**: F2.1 alternate FFT impls (FFTW3 / cuFFT) may close BLAS-vs-FFT gap; F2.2 propagation to end-to-end zero-finding (slot 3 PRIORITY a); F2.3 composite-q via multi-axis FFT (slot 3 PRIORITY b); F2.4 better-tuned BLAS may widen the gap further. **Cross-domain ingredient**: FFT over the dual group of (Z/qZ)* (Bober-Hiary 2017 / Booker-Lobb 2009) USED I — first time in the project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §10 row "FFT over Dirichlet character group" PROPOSED → USED I (constant-factor speedup confirmed but not sub-linear amortisation). Continuation of amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I from Thread 5, now applied at the χ-axis with the cyclic DFT primitive. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; amortised regime unchanged exactly per E1.5), E3.1 (Connes setup-vs-eval decoupling; slot-2 attacks setup), E2.1 (spectral / dual-group structure of (Z/qZ)*), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template — slot-2 implements the χ-axis analogue with bounded wall-clock benefit), S227 (slot-1 baseline directly extended). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot2_afe_shared_l_eval.py, slot2_afe_shared_l_eval_results.md, slot2_l_eval_profile.csv, slot2_scaling.csv, slot2_scaling_quick.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session228_commit_p1_afe_shared_l_eval.md`. | 228 |
