    866:| §P.P1 batched-on-modulus q amortisation, slot 3 — end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder using FFT-shared full AFE (main + reflected via conjugate-of-main identity) + Hardy Z function + sign-change bracketing + linear-interp refinement: does slot-2's L-eval primitive lift to a ≥3× single-query speedup on end-to-end π(x; q, a) computation? | REFINEMENT (architecture + bounded partial-positive at the FFT-vs-DIRECT axis) | I (slot-2's 5× projection FALSIFIED at 1.01×; FFT primitive structurally load-bearing for parity, slot 4-5 attack composite-q + Riemann-Siegel) | Thread 6 commit step 3/5 (S229). Built `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py` (~470 lines): full AFE main term M(t, χ) via slot-2 FFT primitive `phi · ifft(W_aggregate, axis=0)`; reflected term `W_χ · (q/π)^{-it} · ratio_gamma(t, a) · conj(M)` exploiting `Σ χ̄(n)/n^{1/2-it} = conj(Σ χ(n)/n^{1/2+it})` simplification for prime q (NO additional FFT — pointwise multiply); Gauss sums τ(χ_j) via FFT identity in single length-φ FFT; Hardy Z `Z_χ(t) = e^{i·θ(t)} · L(1/2+it, χ)` with `θ_χ(t) = (t/2)·log(q/π) + arg Γ((1/2+a+it)/2) - arg(W_χ)/2` via `np.imag(scipy.special.loggamma)`; vectorised sign-change bracketing across all (φ, n_t) Z values via `np.where(sign(Z[:,:-1]) * sign(Z[:,1:]) < 0)` + linear-interp refinement (9× speedup over per-character Python loop, project tooling fix). N_AFE oversized 12× (without Riemann-Siegel correction terms, balanced N gives error ~1 absolute breaking sign-change detection). Principal character (j=0) uses cached zeta-zeros directly. **Cross-validation against mpmath ground truth**: q=11, χ_1 first 5 zeros: slot-3 (3.55, 6.61, 7.88, 11.60, 13.31) vs mpmath (3.52, 6.64, 7.86, 11.60, 13.35), max diff 0.033; q=11, χ_5 (Legendre) first 4 zeros agree to 0.06 abs. **End-to-end timing q=1009, K=200, x=10⁶, 3-run median**: t_zerofind 151.5ms, t_partialsum 34.8ms, t_single 186.3ms; **1.01× parity vs slot-1 synthetic baseline (187.7ms)**. zeros[median per char] = 135 (out of K_target=200 — n_t=823 grid limited; not all close-spaced zeros captured). **HEADLINE — FFT vs DIRECT no-FFT-sharing**: q=101 K=200 t_max=198 n_t=849 N_afe=678: FFT 43.4ms vs DIRECT 41.1ms = 0.95× (FFT loses at small q); q=1009 K=200 t_max=153 n_t=823 N_afe=1883: **FFT 173.2ms vs DIRECT 352.9ms = 2.04× FFT speedup** (larger than slot-2's L-eval-only 1.13-1.79× because oversized N_afe=1883 widens FFT advantage at large N where BLAS saturates earlier than FFT). Crossover q ∈ [200, 500]. **Internal cost decomposition q=1009 K=200**: setup 0.3ms, AFE 139.3ms (73%), Hardy Z theta 28.2ms (15%), bracket 22.2ms (12%); AFE step dominates. **Amortised at M=256**: slot-3 FFT 35.4ms vs slot-1 23.4ms (slot-3 0.66× slot-1) — T_full_per_x dominated by partial-sum eval (135 real zeros 34.8ms vs slot-1 200 synthetic zeros 22.7ms), FFT primitive does NOT affect partial-sum eval, **exactly per E1.5 prediction**. **WHY slot-2's "5× single-query speedup" projection was wrong**: slot-1's synthetic generator is already cheap (Newton-on-density inversion vectorised numpy: 0.82 µs/gamma); real Dirichlet-L zeros via FFT-Hardy-Z cost 1.1 µs/gamma — only 1.34× more, NOT slower. Plus Hardy theta + bracket costs ~50ms unaccounted-for. **Structural finding (B-grade): real Dirichlet-L zeros for the price of synthetic density-inversion zeros, via cyclic DFT identity + truncated AFE main+reflected**. Not Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped (33× at M=64) but a non-trivial calibration of slot-2's primitive. **Falsifier list F3.1-F3.4 framing slots 4-5**: F3.1 Riemann-Siegel correction terms drop N_afe by 5-10× (Berry 1995 / Coffey 2003) → may push slot-3 from parity to 3-5× partial-positive; F3.2 composite-q multi-axis FFT for q = p₁p₂ (slot 4 PRIORITY-b); F3.3 cross-conductor batches (slot 5); F3.4 better FFT engines (FFTW3/cuFFT) close BLAS-vs-FFT gap. **Cross-domain ingredient**: Hardy Z function for Dirichlet L (Davenport "Multiplicative NT" §15-19 / Booker "Numerical computation of Riemann zeta function") USED I — first end-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder via Hardy Z within the project; CROSS_DOMAIN_TECHNIQUES.md §8 row added. Approximate Functional Equation full+reflected for primitive χ with the conjugate-identity simplification for prime q USED I. scipy.special.loggamma for complex argument (engineering, not new mathematics). Continuation of amortised algorithmics (Tarjan 1985 / Demaine-Patrascu 2008) USED I from Threads 5-6 slot 1-2. Cites E1.5 (per-query bit-content; amortised regime unchanged exactly per E1.5), E2.1 (spectral / sieve interface; cyclic DFT is dual-group spectral primitive), E3.1 (CCM setup-vs-eval decoupling; slot-3 confirms FFT primitive lifts setup but not eval), E6.6 (Aggarwal — orthogonal to slot 3; cross-call vs within-call amortisation), S224 (Correlation Dichotomy template; slot 3 produces real-zeros-for-synthetic-cost shape, smaller than 33× pattern), S227 (slot 1 baseline matched at 1.01×), S228 (slot 2 primitive propagated end-to-end with quantitative falsification of 5× projection). See `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/{slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py, slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet_results.md, slot3_end_to_end.csv, slot3_fft_vs_direct.csv}`, `archive/sessions/session229_commit_p1_dirichlet_zero_finder.md`. | 229 |
