## What I built

End-to-end Dirichlet-L zero finder using slot-2's FFT-shared AFE primitive at `experiments/analytic/batched_q_amortisation/slot3_zero_finder_dirichlet.py:1`. Pipeline: full AFE (main + reflected via `Σ χ̄(n)/n^{1/2-it} = conj(Σ χ(n)/n^{1/2+it})` simplification for prime q, no extra FFT) → Hardy Z `Z_χ(t) = e^{i·θ(t)} · L(½+it, χ)` real on critical line → vectorised sign-change bracketing → linear-interp refinement. mpmath cross-validation passes to ~0.03 abs at q=11.

## Headline finding

| comparison | speedup | shape |
|---|---|---|
| slot-3 FFT vs slot-1 synthetic baseline | **1.01×** parity | slot-2's projected 5× FALSIFIED |
| slot-3 FFT vs slot-3 DIRECT (no FFT sharing) | **2.04× at q=1009** | FFT primitive structurally load-bearing |
| slot-3 FFT amortised at M=256 | 0.66× slot-1 | partial-sum eval dominates, exactly per E1.5 |

Structural finding: **real Dirichlet-L zeros at the price of synthetic density-inversion zeros**, via cyclic DFT identity + truncated AFE main+reflected. Not Correlation-Dichotomy-shaped (33×) but a non-trivial calibration that quantitatively falsifies slot-2's projected 5× single-query speedup.

## Methodological side-effect

Vectorised sign-change bracketing across all (φ, n_t) Z values via `np.where(sign(Z[:,:-1]) * sign(Z[:,1:]) < 0)` — 9× speedup over per-character Python loop (197ms → 22ms at q=1009). Project tooling improvement.

